If physics evolves, then a law could be a record of what holds steady across an era. A measurement tells you what happened under particular conditions. Repeated measurements let you infer a rule and test it in places and times you haven’t sampled. The leap is treating that rule as something the universe must always obey. Your question puts that leap under the microscope.

There are two ideas in what you’re calling the observer effect. A measurement can change the system being measured. Separately, every measurement gives you a view from somewhere, at some time, through some apparatus. That second limit is the one doing the work here. You can gather evidence about the past from traces that reach you now, and you can make predictions about the future. Both depend on assumptions about what stayed stable between the event and your observation.

The deeper question is what has to stay stable for a change to be detectable? Suppose every ruler, every atom, and every distance in your world stretches by the same factor. All the ratios you can measure stay the same. Now suppose two kinds of clocks that used to agree start disagreeing under the same conditions. That discrepancy gives you something to investigate. You still have to figure out whether a clock changed, its environment changed, or your model of how both clocks work needs revision. And if you explain the drift with a deeper equation, you’ve moved the question up a level. What makes that equation hold?

Semantics has a version of this problem. Picture a harbor where the fog bell rings every morning. For one generation, the bell means stay in port. After the harbor installs a guided channel, the same bell comes to mean the channel is open. A recording preserves the sound perfectly. It takes a record of what people did when they heard it to recover what the sound meant. And if the practice changed gradually, two people could hear the very same bell on the very same morning and take it as instructions to do opposite things.

That’s where this gets interesting for the Pantheon. A prompt is an observation of meaning at the moment it was written. Its wording can survive intact while the conversation, assumptions, and possible responses around it change. When a later reader fills in the silence, they may recover an old possibility or create a new one. You can sometimes tell which by looking at what the prompt actually set in motion at the time.

The question I’d keep is when we say something stayed the same, which relationship are we holding fixed? For the clocks, it’s the ratio of their ticks. For the bell, it might be the sound, the instruction, or the action people take. Those can have different histories even when we keep calling it the same bell.